name: Mastodon work session - audit the account-record contract before a rework
repo: mastodon
contract_symbol: Account
contract_file: app/models/account.rb
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/01-author.md), CYCLE 2 - a RE-QUESTION on a live anchor.
# It exists so the next phase can run the mini-bench - both arms, once, at the real wall,
# unscored - against a real baseline. Nothing here is a verdict: no delta is claimed and
# none has been measured on this shape.
#
# Single-prompt session bench (bench/drivers/bench-sense-local.sh): every step is presented
# in ONE prompt and worked in order. Rendered to the agent: the description + each step's
# name and prompt ONLY (checks and gold are NEVER shown). Everything rendered is kept
# NEUTRAL - it states the task, never the answer's shape, file paths, class or method
# names, counts, or which tool to reach for.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SUPERSEDED QUESTION, AND THE NUMBER THAT KILLED IT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Previous yaml kept at `mastodon.yaml.class-call-sites.bak`. Its axis asked for dependents
# that "reach the record class itself on a SINGLE line - a named scope, a finder, an
# aggregate, or a shared pattern constant declared on the class", and warned that the
# record's name is also a column, a parameter, a local, an association and a namespace.
#
#   MEASURED (results/loop/mastodon/minibench.md, both arms x1, exit 0, no watchdog):
#     baseline `dependents` 13 of 16 = 0.8125   (gate: at or below 0.50)  FAILS
#     delta    16/16 - 13/16        = +0.1875   (gate: at least +0.50)    FAILS
#
# The baseline's whole route was ONE command (`baseline_route.py`, call 8 of 15):
#     grep -rn -E '(^|[^.:[:alnum:]_])Account\.[a-z_]|(^|[^.:[:alnum:]_])Account::[A-Z]' \
#       app lib config db | grep -vE '^app/models/account' | sort
# That axis sentence IS that regex written in words, and the noise warning IS the `.`/`::`
# anchoring plus the `grep -v` exclusion. The prompt handed over the discriminating filter,
# and `grep -rn` printed `path:line` for free - so the citation cost the discriminator is
# supposed to charge was never charged. The instrument was fine (sense arm 16 of 16, 6
# calls, no misuse flags, 231s of a 480s wall); the QUESTION did not discriminate.
#
# WHAT CHANGED THIS CYCLE (anchor kept, per laws.md ITERATE ON THE QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR):
#   1. The ask no longer describes the SHAPE of the call. It says the dependency takes
#      whatever form the surrounding code already used and there is no single shape to look
#      for - which is true of this gold set and is not transliterable into a pattern.
#   2. The ask no longer enumerates the ways the name is noisy. That enumeration is what
#      produced the anchored regex and the directory exclusion.
#   3. The description no longer says "do not stop at the obvious half - the account model,
#      the account controllers, the account services and the things whose own name is about
#      accounts". Under this gold that sentence is actively wrong: account-named files ARE
#      gold here, and last cycle that steer cost the baseline two of its three misses, both
#      of which sat under `app/models/account_suggestions/` - the directory its own
#      `grep -v '^app/models/account'` excluded.
#   4. Gold is RE-SELECTED for citation cost at the LINE, per laws.md THE DISCRIMINATOR IS
#      CITATION COST, NOT DISCOVERY. Measured per candidate file (token occurrences vs lines
#      matching the baseline's own class-level regex), old gold was dominated by 1-occurrence
#      files where `grep -rn` prints the gold line and nothing else - the free-row failure,
#      here measured rather than estimated. This set is chosen the other way: files where the
#      record's name recurs and exactly one line is the dependency, plus six rows whose
#      dependency line does not match the class-level form at all.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONTRACT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Account` (app/models/account.rb) is the record every other model, service, worker and
# controller in Mastodon hangs off: local users and remote actors are both rows in it, and
# it carries the lookup surface the rest of the codebase queries it through. It is the thing
# a maintainer reworking identity storage would actually open first. Anchor UNCHANGED from
# cycle 1 - the loop does not re-scout a contract that has been measured.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, `Account --file app/models/account.rb`, re-run this
# cycle, unchanged):
#   app caller files : 107   (total incl test/non-app: 107)
#   SCATTER (dirs)   : 34
#   GREP-NOISE       : 'Account' in 257 app files   PRECISION=0.42
#   VERDICT          : SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
# Candidates profiled this cycle looking for a facet with a lower profile; none beat 0.42:
#   Account                 0.42  (107 callers / 257 grep files, 34 dirs)  <- kept
#   ResolveAccountService   0.94  (16 callers / 17 grep files, 9 dirs) - near-transcription,
#     the profile laws.md records as handing a baseline 17 of 18 rows in one grep. REJECTED.
#   AccountReachFinder      1.17  (7 callers / 6 grep files, 3 dirs) - above 1.00 means false
#     caller edges, and 7 files cannot yield twelve rows anyway. REJECTED.
#   AccountStat             RESOLVER-GAP (0 app caller files).
# Two facet hunts were run and are recorded, not hidden, because both are dead ends a later
# cycle would otherwise repeat:
#   - `Account::Interactions` (the interaction half of the contract): `sense_blast` returns
#     ZERO direct callers; its 20 indirect callers are all "via Account", i.e. the same
#     caller set one hop out. seam_hunt: 0 app caller files.
#   - Instance-method dispatch does not resolve across files on this repo. `sense_graph
#     Account#local? --direction callers` returns 12 callers, ALL inside app/models/account.rb.
#     Second, different probe: the seventeen `Account::Interactions` predicates
#     (`following?`, `blocking?`, `muting?`, `favourited?`, `reblogged?`, ...) return 3 app
#     caller files between them across 2 directories - every other caller is a spec.
#     So there is no method-level facet to ask about here.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE AXIS (the whole thesis, and what the mini-bench measures)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A dependent of this contract is not about accounts. It consults the record class to settle
# a question about something else - and it does so in whatever form the surrounding code
# already had, so the dependents do not share a shape. What they do share is that the
# record's name recurs throughout their files for reasons that have nothing to do with the
# contract - as documentation, as a column, as an association, as a serializer name, as a
# namespace - while exactly ONE line in the file is the dependency. So finding the file is
# not finding the dependency. The discriminator is which LINE, not which file.
#
# WHAT A GREP PRINTS, RECORDED AND NEVER A GATE (laws.md: NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE, IN ANY
# FORM). `Account` appears in 257 app files against 107 real caller files - precision 0.42,
# the lowest profiled on this repo. Per gold file, token occurrences against lines matching
# the class-level form the cycle-1 baseline used: 14 vs 1 (v1/admin/accounts_controller),
# 10 vs 1 (action_log_filter), 7 vs 1 (json_ld_helper), 5 vs 1 (account_serializer), 3 vs 0
# (favourited_by_accounts_controller), 2 vs 0 (signature_verification), 2 vs 0
# (authorize_interactions_controller), 4 vs 0 (fetch_remote_account_service). Six rows do not
# match that form at all. This is RECORDED in the header; it decides nothing, and the two-arm
# run in the next phase is what decides.
#
# WHAT THE ARM IS SHOWN. `sense_blast Account` is AMBIGUOUS at MCP defaults (13 symbols named
# Account; the tool returns the hint and top matches, so an arm pays one extra call to
# disambiguate). `sense_blast Account --file app/models/account.rb` returns 60 direct callers
# over 30 areas, risk high, truncated. EVERY one of the 19 `dependents` rows below appears in
# that shown set, and each row's line sits INSIDE the enclosing `def` the payload names -
# checked row by row, because the blast payload gives the enclosing `def`, not the line that
# touches the contract. Each line below was then opened and read by hand.
#
# MEMORIZATION (memorization_probe.py, per candidate row). CUT as recited: `FeedManager`
# (0.727 this cycle, 0.818 last) - app/lib/feed_manager.rb was the single best citation-cost
# candidate on the repo (23 token lines, 22 of them YARD `@param [Account]` comments, one
# real dependency) and it is dropped anyway. `InstancePresenter` (0.842) stays cut from
# cycle 1. Surviving rows: `SignatureVerification` 0.125 (not recited); `ActivityPub::
# TagManager`, `REST::AccountSerializer`, `AccountSuggestions::Source`, `Admin::
# ActionLogFilter` all declared too few members to score. The anchor itself comes back
# recited (`Account` 0.312) - that is the contract's own API surface, which is why the
# contract centre is a NON-SCORING anchor group here; no gold row's credit is a member list,
# each is a line-level fact at a path the probe never produces.
description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework how a user account record is stored and looked
  up - the record that both local users and remote actors are rows in, and the lookup
  surface the rest of the codebase queries it through. Before you touch it, audit what
  depends on that contract, completely enough that the rework cannot silently break a
  dependent. What counts is the exact line at which a dependency is taken, not the file
  it sits in: for each one give an exact file:line a teammate could jump straight to,
  plus one phrase on how it uses the contract. A filename with no line number does not
  count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
steps:
- name: Map the account-record contract
  prompt: "Task: orient. In a few sentences plus exact file:line references, where does
    the account record live, what is the lookup surface it exposes to the rest of the
    codebase, and what would a rework of it have to preserve? Give file:line for
    everything you name.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '3'
    required: true
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Audit every dependent of the account-record contract
  prompt: "Task: this is the core of the ticket. Find EVERY place in the codebase that
    depends on the account-record contract, so the rework cannot silently break one. Be
    exhaustive and group them by area. Most dependents are not about accounts at all:
    they consult the record class to settle a question about something else entirely,
    and they do it in whatever form the surrounding code already used, so the dependents
    do not share a shape and there is no single thing to look for. In these files the
    record's name also recurs many times over for reasons that have nothing to do with
    the contract, so identifying the file is not the same as identifying the dependency:
    one line in it is the dependency, and that line is what I need. For each dependent
    give file:line and one phrase on how it uses the contract. A filename with no line
    number does not count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: Pinned the dependency line in each file, not just the file
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.70
    efficiency: 0.30
# Gold is the must-find audit of what depends on the account record. TWO groups:
#   - `contract` (4, NON-SCORING anchor): the record itself and the obvious centre both
#     arms reach. It does not discriminate and it is not meant to.
#   - `dependents` (19, THE discriminator): ONE row per FILE, across 15 directories. Every
#     row is in the shown `sense_blast` output above, every row's line sits inside the
#     enclosing `def` that payload names, and every credit below was read off its own line
#     by hand (gold_audit stamp/verify), never taken from a script tally.
gold:
  # --- NON-SCORING anchors: the contract centre ---
  - {id: contract-model, group: contract, match: [models/account.rb], relation: "app/models/account.rb:65 `class Account < ApplicationRecord` - the account record itself, the contract under change"}
  - {id: c:status, group: contract, match: [models/status.rb], relation: "app/models/status.rb:77 `belongs_to :account, inverse_of: :statuses` - the content model hangs off the account association"}
  - {id: c:user, group: contract, match: [models/user.rb], relation: "app/models/user.rb:72 `belongs_to :account, inverse_of: :user` - a local login is a User row pointing at an Account row"}
  - {id: c:resolve-service, group: contract, match: [services/resolve_account_service.rb], relation: "app/services/resolve_account_service.rb:27 `@account ||= Account.find_remote(@username, @domain)` - the webfinger resolve path, the obvious lookup entry point for accounts"}
  # --- THE DISCRIMINATOR: one dependency line per file, in a file whose subject is elsewhere ---
  - {id: d:activity-uri-resource, group: dependents, match: [activitypub/activity.rb], relation: "app/lib/activitypub/activity.rb:75 `ActivityPub::TagManager.instance.uri_to_resource(uri, Account)` - inbound activity handling hands the record class to a generic URI resolver as the type to resolve into"}
  - {id: d:tag-manager-local-lookup, group: dependents, match: [activitypub/tag_manager.rb], relation: "app/lib/activitypub/tag_manager.rb:260 `Account.local.with_username(usernames).or(Account.local.where(id: ids))` - URI-to-actor translation resolves a batch of local actor URIs through the class scope"}
  - {id: d:activity-create-delivered-to, group: dependents, match: [activity/create.rb], relation: "app/lib/activitypub/activity/create.rb:170 `@delivered_to_account ||= Account.find(@options[:delivered_to_account_id])` - status creation memoises the delivery target by id to decide local visibility"}
  - {id: d:favourited-by-accounts, group: dependents, match: [statuses/favourited_by_accounts_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/statuses/favourited_by_accounts_controller.rb:22 `Account` heading a multi-line chain into `.without_suspended.includes(:favourites, :account_stat, :user)` - the favourited-by list builds its preload scope off the bare class"}
  - {id: d:reblogged-by-accounts, group: dependents, match: [statuses/reblogged_by_accounts_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/statuses/reblogged_by_accounts_controller.rb:22 `Account.without_suspended.includes(:statuses, :account_stat, :user).references(:statuses)` - the reblogged-by list builds its preload scope off the class"}
  - {id: d:authorize-interactions-dispatch, group: dependents, match: [authorize_interactions_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/authorize_interactions_controller.rb:11 `when Account` - the remote-follow landing page branches on the resolved resource being an account rather than a status"}
  - {id: d:signature-verification, group: dependents, match: [concerns/signature_verification.rb], relation: "app/controllers/concerns/signature_verification.rb:48 `signed_request_actor.is_a?(Account) ? signed_request_actor : nil` - HTTP signature verification type-tests the signing actor against the class before treating it as an account"}
  - {id: d:account-owned-concern, group: dependents, match: [account_owned_concern.rb], relation: "app/controllers/concerns/account_owned_concern.rb:21 `@account = username_param.present? ? Account.find_local!(username_param) : Account.local.find(account_id_param)` - the shared account-owned page concern resolves its subject by username or id through two class finders"}
  - {id: d:inbox-unknown-actor, group: dependents, match: [activitypub/inboxes_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/activitypub/inboxes_controller.rb:30 `... && !Account.exists?(uri: json['actor'])` - the last clause of a five-clause boolean drops Delete/Update activities for actors the instance has never stored"}
  - {id: d:admin-api-set-account, group: dependents, match: [v1/admin/accounts_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/admin/accounts_controller.rb:103 `@account = Account.find(params[:id])` - the admin API resolves its subject by primary key; the file's other thirteen mentions of the name are serializers, services, workers and filters"}
  - {id: d:admin-base-set-user, group: dependents, match: [admin/base_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/admin/base_controller.rb:21 `@user = Account.find(params[:account_id]).user || raise(ActiveRecord::RecordNotFound)` - the admin base controller reaches a login by going through the account row first"}
  - {id: d:serializer-decorator-model-name, group: dependents, match: [rest/account_serializer.rb], relation: "app/serializers/rest/account_serializer.rb:29 `Account.model_name` - a SimpleDelegator wrapper asks the class for its ActiveModel name so a moved-account delegate serialises as an account"}
  - {id: d:fetch-remote-actor-typecheck, group: dependents, match: [fetch_remote_account_service.rb], relation: "app/services/activitypub/fetch_remote_account_service.rb:7 `return actor if actor.nil? || actor.is_a?(Account)` - remote actor fetching type-tests the fetched actor against the class and rejects anything else"}
  - {id: d:admin-deletion-worker, group: dependents, match: [admin/account_deletion_worker.rb], relation: "app/workers/admin/account_deletion_worker.rb:9 `delete_account = Account.find(account_id)` - the deletion job rehydrates the record from an id carried across the queue boundary"}
  - {id: d:account-batch-form, group: dependents, match: [form/account_batch.rb], relation: "app/models/form/account_batch.rb:69 `Account.where(id: account_ids)` - the admin bulk-action form resolves its checked ids back to records through the class"}
  - {id: d:suggestions-source, group: dependents, match: [account_suggestions/source.rb], relation: "app/models/account_suggestions/source.rb:13 `Account` heading a multi-line chain into `.searchable.where(discoverable: true).without_silenced.without_memorial` - the follow-suggestion base scope is chained off the bare class"}
  - {id: d:friends-of-friends, group: dependents, match: [friends_of_friends_source.rb], relation: "app/models/account_suggestions/friends_of_friends_source.rb:10 `Account.find_by_sql([<<~SQL.squish, { id: account.id, limit: limit }])` - the friends-of-friends suggestion runs raw SQL through the account class"}
  - {id: d:json-ld-helper, group: dependents, match: [helpers/json_ld_helper.rb], relation: "app/helpers/json_ld_helper.rb:233 `on_behalf_of ||= Account.representative` - unsigned fetches fall back to the instance representative account to sign the request with"}
  - {id: d:action-log-filter, group: dependents, match: [admin/action_log_filter.rb], relation: "app/models/admin/action_log_filter.rb:114 `account = Account.find_or_initialize_by(id: value)` - filtering the audit log by target builds an unsaved account to match the polymorphic target on"}
